{"name":"Geraldine","hex":"#e77b75","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Geraldine","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A female given name from the Germanic languages.","A town in South Canterbury, New Zealand, named after the FitzGerald family."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to the medieval FitzGerald dynasty of Ireland."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Geraldine"}],"entries":[{"title":"Geraldine (name)","blocks":[{"text":"Geraldine is a feminine form of the first name Gerald. Notable people with the name include:"},{"items":["Geraldine Aves (1898–1986), British civil servant","Geraldine Brannigan, Irish singer","Geraldine Chaplin, American actress","Geraldine Connor (1952–2011), British ethnomusicologist, theatre director, composer and performer","Geraldine L. Daniels (1933–2012), New York politician","Geraldine Farrar (1882–1967), American opera soprano","Geraldine Ferraro (1935–2011), United States congresswoman and 1984 vice presidential candidate","Geraldine Fitzgerald (1913–2005), Irish actress","Geraldina Guerra Garcés, Ecuadorian campaigner against femicide","Geraldine Hakewill, Australian actress","Geri Halliwell, former Spice Girl","Geraldine James, English actress","Geraldine Laybourne, American television executive","Géraldine Martineau, French actress","Geraldine McCaughrean, British author","Geraldine McCormick (1924–2005), American politician","Geraldine McEwan (1932–2015), English actress","Geraldine O'Grady (1932–2025), Irish classical violinist","Geraldine Page (1924–1987), American actress","Geraldine Roman, Filipino journalist and politician","Geraldine Smith (actress)","Geraldine Smith (politician)","Geraldine Somerville, Irish actress","Geraldine Viswanathan, Australian actress"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geraldine_(name)"},{"title":"FitzGerald dynasty","blocks":[{"text":"FitzGerald is a Hiberno-Norman noble and aristocratic dynasty, originally of Cambro-Norman and Anglo-Norman origin. They have been peers of Ireland since at least the 13th century, and are described in the Annals of the Four Masters as having become \"more Irish than the Irish themselves\" due to assimilation with the native Gaelic aristocratic and popular culture. The dynasty has also been referred to as the Geraldines and Ireland's largest landowners. They achieved power through the conquest and colonisation of large swathes of Irish territory by the sons and grandsons of Gerald de Windsor, the first Castellan of Pembroke Castle in Wales and progenitor of the FitzMaurice and FitzGerald dynasties. The family name means \"sons of Gerald\", with \"fitz\" from Anglo-Norman fils \"son\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Duke_of_Leinster.svg/330px-Duke_of_Leinster.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FitzGerald_dynasty"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Windsor_Castle_at_Sunset_-_Nov_2006.jpg/500px-Windsor_Castle_at_Sunset_-_Nov_2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Windsor_Castle_at_Sunset_-_Nov_2006.jpg/1280px-Windsor_Castle_at_Sunset_-_Nov_2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Windsor_Castle_at_Sunset_-_Nov_2006.jpg/500px-Windsor_Castle_at_Sunset_-_Nov_2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Windsor Castle, a residence of William the Conqueror first held by Gerald de Windsor's father and brother","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWindsor_Castle_at_Sunset_-_Nov_2006.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Carew_Castle_%286744540501%29.jpg/500px-Carew_Castle_%286744540501%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Carew_Castle_%286744540501%29.jpg/1280px-Carew_Castle_%286744540501%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Carew_Castle_%286744540501%29.jpg/500px-Carew_Castle_%286744540501%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Carew Castle, initially built by Gerald de Windsor, estate part of Princess Nest dowry","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACarew_Castle_(6744540501).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Carton_House_IE20100403_007_maynooth.jpg/500px-Carton_House_IE20100403_007_maynooth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Carton_House_IE20100403_007_maynooth.jpg/1280px-Carton_House_IE20100403_007_maynooth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Carton_House_IE20100403_007_maynooth.jpg/500px-Carton_House_IE20100403_007_maynooth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Carton House was the ancestral seat for over 700 years of the Dukes of Leinster","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACarton_House_IE20100403_007_maynooth.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Ireland_1450.png/330px-Ireland_1450.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Ireland_1450.png/330px-Ireland_1450.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ireland in 1450, showing the Geraldine earldoms of Kildare and Desmond","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIreland_1450.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Berkley_Sq%2C_London_W1_%2825954583006%29.jpg/500px-Berkley_Sq%2C_London_W1_%2825954583006%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Berkley_Sq%2C_London_W1_%2825954583006%29.jpg/1280px-Berkley_Sq%2C_London_W1_%2825954583006%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Berkley_Sq%2C_London_W1_%2825954583006%29.jpg/500px-Berkley_Sq%2C_London_W1_%2825954583006%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lansdowne House, London seat of the Marquess of Lansdowne, was later occupied by William Waldorf Astor, and Hannah de Rothschild of Mentmore Towers.[20] It was also the location of the draft of the Treaty of Paris, which gave independence to the United States.[21]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABerkley_Sq%2C_London_W1_(25954583006).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Maurice_FitzGerald%2C_Lord_Lanstephan.jpg/500px-Maurice_FitzGerald%2C_Lord_Lanstephan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Maurice_FitzGerald%2C_Lord_Lanstephan.jpg/500px-Maurice_FitzGerald%2C_Lord_Lanstephan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Maurice FitzGerald, Lord of Lanstephan, progenitor of the Irish Geraldines, from a manuscript of the Expugnatio Hibernica, an account of the 1169 invasion of Ireland written by Maurice's nephew, Gerald of Wales, in 1189.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMaurice_FitzGerald%2C_Lord_Lanstephan.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Duke_of_Leinster_coa.png/500px-Duke_of_Leinster_coa.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=